#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199425} :activity/id #uuid "572640c2-48d2-5508-9b8f-be1309fc61e3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049076 :topic/id #uuid "d8ad0390-dfee-58d8-ac50-00cbfdc660af" :topic/math-academy-id 4393} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199577 :tutorial/content "As previously seen, we can define sets using so-called *constructive definitions* of the form\n\n$$\n\\big\\{ {\\color{blue}\\textrm{rule}} \\: : \\: {\\color{blue}\\textrm{base set}} \\big\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere the ${\\color{blue}\\textrm{rule}}$ is an expression (or formula) that depends on a variable that *constructs* elements of our set when the variable runs over some ${\\color{blue}\\textrm{base set}}.$ \n\nFor example, the set $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA = \\big\\{ 2n+1\\, : \\, n\\in\\mathbb N_0 \\big \\}\n$$\n\nis defined constructively and contains all positive odd numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={2n+1\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}} \\\\  & ={2(0)+1,\\,2(1)+1,\\,2(2)+1,\\,2(3)+1,\\,…} \\\\  & ={1,3,5,7,…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSometimes, it's helpful to write a set using a constructive definition. For example, how might we express the following set constructively?\n\n$$\nB = \\big\\{ 4, 7, 10, 13, \\ldots \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the elements of our set \n\n$$\n4,\\quad 7,\\quad 10,\\quad 13,\\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nform an arithmetic sequence with common difference $d=3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =3 \\\\  & =7-4 \\\\  & =10-7 \\\\  & =13-10 \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term $a_n$ of our sequence for $n\\in\\mathbb N$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)\\cdot d \\\\  & =4+(n-1)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =4+3n-3 \\\\  & =3n+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set constructively as follows:\n\n$$\nB = \\big\\{ 3n+1 \\: : \\: n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe expression $3n+1$ generates the elements of our set when $n$ runs over $\\mathbb{N}.$ \n\nNote the following:\n\n- A bar \"$\\mid$\" is just as acceptable as a colon to separate the rule from the base set. So, we could also express our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB=\\big\\{ 3n+1 \\: \\mid \\: n \\in \\mathbb{N} \\big\\}\n$$\n\n- The constructive definition of a given set is typically not unique. For example, we could also define $B$ as follows:\n\n$$\nB=\\big\\{ 3n+4 \\: :\\: n \\in \\mathbb{N}_0 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used $\\mathbb N_0$ as the base set.\n\nThis definition does indeed generate all elements of $B{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3n+4\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}} & ={3(0)+4,\\,3(1)+4,\\,3(2)+4,\\,3(3)+4,\\,…} \\\\  & ={4,\\,7,\\,10,\\,13,\\,…} \\\\  & =B.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "57358da7-7aa7-5201-b3bd-e43581b18a41" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8307 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "95633ec2-0a87-53d4-9845-589ad5e2c472" :step/math-academy-id 67909 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199426}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199426 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198463 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198166 :question/id #uuid "231da77b-8370-543a-abf0-effb78e50802" :question/math-academy-id "e-13186"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5b3eb6-187f-5f16-a32e-66b0842adfe9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050157} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198872} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287858} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287860} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396295}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Given Set Using an Arithmetic Sequence"} :step/id #uuid "d31b48cd-0396-5a79-94df-84872a357ae8" :step/math-academy-id 67910 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199427}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199427 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198464 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198164 :question/id #uuid "e81a9a3f-3dbb-5244-8ea0-9b967bb5a46f" :question/math-academy-id "e-13184"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9df722a8-2db6-5bb8-a69c-b81b33037ab2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050067}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287863} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396308}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Given Set Using a Geometric Sequence"} :step/id #uuid "01644308-2657-5bfc-bdad-7c6353f7293a" :step/math-academy-id 67913 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199428}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199428 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199552 :tutorial/content "We can also define sets using so-called *conditional definitions* of the form\n\n$$\n\\{ {\\color{blue}\\textrm{base set}} \\: : \\: {\\color{blue}\\textrm{conditions}} \\}\n$$\n\nwhere our set contains only those elements of the ${\\color{blue}\\textrm{base set}}$ that satisfy the given ${\\color{blue}\\textrm{conditions}}$ (or restrictions).\n\nFor example, the set $A,$ given by\n\n$$\nA = \\{n\\in \\mathbb Z \\::\\: -2\\leq n\\leq 2\\}\n$$\n\nis defined conditionally and contains all integers between $-2$ and $2$ inclusive:\n\n$$\nA = \\{-2,-1,0,1,2\\}\n$$\n\nIt's often helpful to describe sets using conditional definitions. For example, let's rewrite the following set using a conditional definition.\n\n$$\nS = \\big\\{50,51,52,53,\\ldots\\big\\}\n$$\n\nThe set $S$ contains all natural numbers greater than or equal to $50,$ which we can write as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\{n\\in \\mathbb N \\::\\: n\\geq 50\\}\n$$\n\nThe conditional definition of a set is not unique. We can write down an equivalent definition of $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\nS = \\{n\\in \\mathbb N \\::\\: n\\gt 49\\}\n$$\n\nUnder this definition, $S$ contains all natural numbers greater than $49,$ *excluding* $49.$ \n\nAlthough $\\mathbb N$ is the most natural base set in this instance, we can also define $S$ using the set of integers $\\mathbb Z$ as the base set. For example,\n\n$$\nS = \\{n\\in \\mathbb Z \\::\\: n\\geq 50\\}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a5b5e889-bdb2-5707-90ab-be875064cc73" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8228 :tutorial/title "Conditional Definitions of Sets"} :step/id #uuid "82de79d7-d2f1-5a78-845b-a3a4a47ee4c5" :step/math-academy-id 67916 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199429}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199429 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198465 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198150 :question/id #uuid "cfd5a7b6-4ee8-5c66-9b1d-81d1d9a1aee7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13144"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f49ef1c2-06de-597c-bf4d-6d613989987e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050035}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396272} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412792}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Set Using a Conditional Definition"} :step/id #uuid "5ed9a110-74bf-5cdf-8e45-8f23b994a145" :step/math-academy-id 67917 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199430}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199430 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198466 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198165 :question/id #uuid "a67f56bb-352b-5126-84c8-c38fc3d0532c" :question/math-academy-id "e-13185"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad21350b-2e3b-57ee-a28c-32a7bd1b2513" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049645} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050455}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287857}] :knowledge-point/title "Defining a Set Conditionally Using the Roots of a Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "03fd2408-ced4-5196-8d53-1d732ef57031" :step/math-academy-id 67920}] :activity/title "Describing Sets Using Set-Builder Notation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198534}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418462} :activity/id #uuid "b1628e49-8c52-59b4-abeb-efcedb34a064" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049076 :topic/id #uuid "d8ad0390-dfee-58d8-ac50-00cbfdc660af" :topic/math-academy-id 4393} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418461 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198844 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6756d5e9-8c2b-5648-9cef-d57cf02e8e50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {2}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396245} {:answer/id #uuid "de50addf-3149-5f58-b066-97f2f40179d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\cdot {2}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396246} {:answer/id #uuid "cf715dcc-e03e-5ea8-aeed-f7ac4fdc33cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {3}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396247} {:answer/id #uuid "7855bcc5-306f-5477-83df-730bda1dbba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {3}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396248} {:answer/id #uuid "9b1a1661-077d-5469-a87e-386432d18ff0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{2}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396249}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396246} :answer-field/id #uuid "3da75658-493e-5414-9ef4-07db0edd9ac6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396244} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02e44344-d16a-5d1d-af75-d63577743717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396251} {:answer/id #uuid "717cb1ad-26fb-5d39-b775-f38b2206a689" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396252} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2860f0-b7f1-553d-a57e-50270b5ec131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396253}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396252} :answer-field/id #uuid "95707686-21cc-5e61-98a8-5364df18a6b6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396250}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4a35af51-a0e2-50d0-bbd3-1c0dc3bc52aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-183328" :question/problem "Describe the set $S=\\{…,3,6,12,24,48,...\\}$ using a constructive definition.\n\n*Hint: Take ${a}_{1}=6$ in the sequence that generates the elements of $S.$*\n\n$$\nS=\\{{{field-1}}\\,:\\,n\\in {{field-2}}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the elements of our set\n\n$$\n…,\\,3,\\,6,\\,12,\\,24,\\,48,\\,…\n$$\n\nform a two-way infinite geometric sequence with a common ratio $r=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =2 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{24}{12} \\\\  & =⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting  ${a}_{1}=6,$ the $n$th term ${a}_{n}$ of our sequence for $n\\in Z$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =6\\cdot {2}^{n-1} \\\\  & =6\\cdot {2}^{n}\\cdot {2}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2}\\cdot {2}^{n} \\\\  & =3\\cdot {2}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can define our set using a constructive definition as follows:\n\n$$\nS=\\{3\\cdot {2}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in Z\\}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "07b065de-bc57-5b1f-9638-2357e81c045e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418465}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418462 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58adad53-8ca3-58fb-98eb-57482e4bd46a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390161} {:answer/id #uuid "c593d39e-6133-5c10-a20c-02e7b771c723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390162} {:answer/id #uuid "4492d437-9549-5ca9-9bb2-c2bbce7b9eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390163} {:answer/id #uuid "0d0e134c-e4a1-57d7-a9af-e0a0da4d34fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "49" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390164} {:answer/id #uuid "ed4e7255-b961-5a90-96f5-485fd201979d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390165}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390162} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b58d997-a72e-5be2-a36a-0dcd6a3375be" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390160} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7af87b5-4719-5d92-8753-f1c449d69fb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390167} {:answer/id #uuid "15d191a0-12ac-57d4-8409-2a22f927c78e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390168} {:answer/id #uuid "e8584b9a-78d4-5f94-88fa-976f6ca2d98d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390169} {:answer/id #uuid "b117c203-c518-5253-90da-b24f9e8313f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390170}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390167} :answer-field/id #uuid "73ac03b3-1352-5553-b113-c375cbc92ca3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390166} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac85e5cb-3cb1-5a31-b54b-92e219c3e087" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390172} {:answer/id #uuid "8128199d-8517-5eef-a7db-2060abe61c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390173} {:answer/id #uuid "6aba1d10-949d-5fdd-8086-a4c20b3b4fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390174} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c9cd45-fea5-585c-afd0-1e780adb1385" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390175} {:answer/id #uuid "088fb8e1-80c8-5b96-9fd5-7e059d4a9db8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390176}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390176} :answer-field/id #uuid "a223d6ba-7747-5202-9cbc-db253e2e4893" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390171}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "55adfa30-bde9-5dd7-8854-b990477f8d63" :question/math-academy-id "q-183325" :question/problem "Describe the set $S=\\{5,6,7,...,50\\}$ using a conditional definition.\n\n$$\nS=\\{x\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,{{field-2}}\\le x\\le {{field-3}}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The set $S$ contains all natural numbers between $5$ and $50$ (including $5$ and $50$).\n\nTherefore, the set can be written as\n\n$$\nS=\\{x\\in N\\,:\\,5\\le x\\le 50\\}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: This definition is not unique. For example:\n\n- We can write $x\\in Z.$ This gives\n\n$$\nS=\\{x\\in Z\\,:\\,5\\le x\\le 50\\}.\n$$\n\n- We can write $4<x<51.$ This gives\n\n$$\nS=\\{x\\in N\\,:\\,4<x<51\\},\n$$\n\nor, writing $x\\in Z$ as before, we have\n\n$$\nS=\\{x\\in Z\\,:\\,4<x<51\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8aac6117-dcf7-586f-a196-0f59c7458408" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418461}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418463 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8def1d3c-eb5a-566b-af12-d3ce42da3e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412787} {:answer/id #uuid "9c500ffe-51a8-5905-8476-0b724f9d7bf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412788} {:answer/id #uuid "f774e7f6-8dc4-55a7-a225-c1111386f024" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412789}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412787} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b480447-2056-5cc5-bd5d-e625edb62fa3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412790} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c80075a-23bd-565b-b792-75b9483248b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412783} {:answer/id #uuid "1fec48bf-7341-5e00-bf54-1e2e3c7d845f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "N" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412784} {:answer/id #uuid "2ff79e69-0b12-56e7-9984-59ab4023180c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412785} {:answer/id #uuid "3b1ceaab-4f04-5722-81a1-b363e51746d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412786}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412785} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddcd27c7-8319-5553-8fa5-9d996008549a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412791}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1e8c26e7-e9d8-563e-b578-6100bf923d6c" :question/math-academy-id "q-183697" :question/problem "Describe the set $S=\\{…,-2,-1,0,1,2,3\\}$ using a conditional definition.\n\n$$\nS=\\{x\\in {{field-1}}\\,:\\,x<{{field-2}}\\}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The set $S$ contains all integers less than $3,$ including $3.$\n\nIn other words, $S$ contains all integers less than $4,$  *excluding* $4.$ \n\nTherefore, the set can be written as\n\n$$\nS=\\{x\\in Z\\,:\\,x<4\\}.\n$$\n\n**Note**: This definition is not unique. For example:\n\n- We can write $x\\le 3.$ This gives\n\n$$\nS=\\{x\\in Z\\,:\\,x\\le 3\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "97d9e6fc-8a81-5514-9e30-4a41f4e00265" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418464}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418464 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287857 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bcff3a1a-a2f3-5bdb-ac79-6dd44d5cc596" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286512}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286512} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d58c939-488c-5ba6-9cbb-2a5e7499c7be" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287212} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a5f4dcf-8fe9-57f1-adc1-722c8f879243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286202}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186286202} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e85f64d-db61-5514-b2c7-a9a8b11f7190" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287214} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fdec9f3-318f-51f1-85f9-84a5f248750f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285667}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285667} :answer-field/id #uuid "8eb3f8c2-6388-5345-9c70-eb27eeba4550" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "250dc34e-ac8d-50ac-8f72-1123fa8a7b34" :question/math-academy-id "q-183751" :question/problem "Find the missing coefficients in the conditional definition of the set $\\{-3,-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}\\}$ given below.\n\n$\\{-3,-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}\\}=\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,3{x}^{3}\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n${x}^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$x\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$=0\\}$" :question/worked-solution "The given set contains the roots $x\\in R$ of some cubic polynomial $p(x).$\n\nSince the cubic polynomial has roots ${x}_{1}=-3,$ ${x}_{2}=-\\sqrt{5},$ and ${x}_{3}=\\sqrt{5},$ and the coefficient of the cubic term is $a=3,$ we can write the polynomial as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =a(x-{x}_{1})(x-{x}_{2})(x-{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =(3)(x-(-3))(x-(-\\sqrt{5}))(x-\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =3(x+3)(x+\\sqrt{5})(x-\\sqrt{5}) \\\\  & =3(x+3)({x}^{2}-\\sqrt{5}x+\\sqrt{5}x-(\\sqrt{5})^{2}) \\\\  & =3(x+3)({x}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =3({x}^{3}-5x+3{x}^{2}-15) \\\\  & =3{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}-15x-45\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the given set as\n\n$$\n\\{-3,-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}\\}=\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,3{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}-15x-45=0\\}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a5125736-78d1-5780-b300-7ef91791e033"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186418465 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b665804-beaa-5388-ac25-9397d16709cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396256} {:answer/id #uuid "cc29d216-b4b5-5d05-aee8-fa1d64f964da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396257} {:answer/id #uuid "80b0d143-f228-52ab-ad57-54619241a5e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "Z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396258} {:answer/id #uuid "20f47bfb-da3d-5440-aa25-5e8189b5f62d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "R" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396259}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396258} :answer-field/id #uuid "474b1fda-a88a-5ba4-9077-194e8d8272f7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396255} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c59aee3-d837-5b8a-89d8-81c0f7ee8818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186396261} {:answer/id #uuid "684cccbb-1991-5da7-b5d8-ae5038ca20fb" :answer/type {